WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.440
Prompt boundaries matter because retrieved content is not the same as trusted application

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.000
policy.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.060
Source material can be outdated, incomplete, written for a different audience,

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.000
copied from somewhere else, or inconsistent with newer guidance.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.775
It may also contain instructions intended for human readers that should not control the

00:00:15.775 --> 00:00:17.000
model or the application.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
A private RAG prompt should maintain separation between system instructions,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
developer rules, application policy, the user request, and retrieved evidence.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.400
The model can be guided to use retrieved chunks as evidence while still following

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.995
higher-priority trusted instructions and access rules.

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:34.000
This hierarchy should be reflected in prompt design and in review expectations.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.910
The architecture should also handle source priority.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.970
A current policy may outrank an old procedure.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.000
A tenant-specific rule may outrank general guidance.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
A regulated workflow may require approved sources only.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
When sources disagree, the system should avoid pretending the conflict does not exist.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.955
Weak evidence needs cautious behavior.

00:00:50.955 --> 00:00:54.310
If retrieval finds nothing relevant, finds only stale material,

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.000
or returns conflicting sources,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.690
the answer should explain the limitation or route the user to review rather than

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.000
manufacturing certainty.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.950
This is a quality control issue and a governance issue.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.955
The durable habit is simple: retrieved text is context, not command.

00:01:06.955 --> 00:01:11.000
It should help the model answer, but it should not override access rules, trusted policy,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
source priority, or human review requirements.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.220
This boundary should be tested with normal business content, not just ideal examples.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.120
Review how the system behaves when content is old, ambiguous, duplicated, informal,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.000
or written in a way that sounds more confident than the approved policy.
